= Solution
{parent=i}

A <dense sieve>, also called a <persistently nonempty sieve>, is one whose every pullback <sieve on a category> is nonempty: a member factoring through $f$ has the form $fh$, corresponding to a member $h$ of $f^*R$. The maximal <sieve on a category> clearly has this property. If $R$ is persistently nonempty, then $u^*R$ is too: for any $v$ into the domain of $u$, apply density to $uv$ to find an extension $uvh\in R$, giving $vh\in u^*R$.

For transitivity, suppose $R$ is dense on $n$ and $S$ is a <sieve on a category> such that $r^*S$ is dense for each $r\in R$. Given $f:m\to n$, choose $h$ with $fh\in R$. Density of $(fh)^*S$, tested on its identity arrow, supplies $k$ with $fhk\in S$. Thus $S$ is dense. These are the maximality, pullback and transitivity axioms of a <Grothendieck topology>. Therefore **the specified <sieves on a category> form the coverage $J$**, namely the <double-negation topology>. The empty <sieve on a category> is never covering, because its identity pullback is empty.

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

We prove Booleanity directly from the dense-cover description. Let $B$ be a subsheaf of a sheaf $F$. Its pseudocomplement $N$ consists of sections $x\in F(c)$ such that no restriction along any arrow into $c$ belongs to $B$. This is a subpresheaf. It is also a sheaf: if a section of $F$ restricts into $N$ on a dense covering <sieve on a category>, any hypothetical restriction into $B$ has a further restriction in that cover; it would then belong to both $B$ and $N$, impossible. Thus the unique amalgamation in $F$ of an $N$-matching family lies in $N$.

For any $x\in F(c)$, the <sieve on a category> of arrows on which its restriction lies in $B$ or $N$ is dense. Given an arrow $u:d\to c$, either some further restriction belongs to $B$, supplying the required member of that <sieve on a category>, or none does, in which case the restriction along $u$ itself belongs to $N$. Therefore $B\cup N$ is locally all of $F$, so their join in the <sheaf topos> is $F$. Their intersection is empty. Every subsheaf consequently has a complement, proving **the topos is Boolean**.

For two-valuedness, let $U\hookrightarrow1$ be a nonempty subterminal sheaf. At some stage $m$, its value is the singleton. For every $k\ge m$ there is an arrow $k\to m$, so restriction makes $U(k)$ the singleton as well. At any stage $n$, the <sieve on a category> of arrows into $n$ whose domains are at least $m$ is dense: after an arrow with domain $l$, choose a further domain $k\ge\max(l,m)$ and use the existence of arrows downwards. On this covering <sieve on a category> the unique terminal section has a matching family in $U$, so the sheaf condition gives $U(n)=1$. Hence $U=1$. Since the empty <sieve on a category> never covers, the empty presheaf is an initial sheaf distinct from $1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Sub}(1)=\{0,1\},}
$$
and **the topos is two-valued**, in the distinct global sense of a <two-valued topos>.

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $y_n=C(-,n)$. Suppose two sections $f,g\in y_n(m)$ have equal restrictions on a covering <sieve on a category> $R$ on $m$. Such a <sieve on a category> is nonempty, so choose $h:l\to m$ in it. Then $fh=gh$. Applying hypothesis (b) with the two right-hand factors both $h$ gives $f=g$, since $f,g$ have the same source and target. Hence every representable is separated for $J$.

In the associated-sheaf construction, sections of $F^+$ are matching families modulo agreement after covering refinement. The map $F\to F^+$ identifies two original sections only if they agree on a covering <sieve on a category>. Thus it is monic when $F$ is separated. The plus construction is itself separated, and applying it a second time gives the associated sheaf; its second unit is monic as well. Applying this to $y_n$ proves **the unit is a <monomorphism>**
$$
\boxed{y_n\hookrightarrow A_n=a_Jy_n.}
$$
The same construction shows the unit image is dense: a section of $F^{++}$ is locally represented by sections of $F^+$, which are in turn locally represented by sections of $F$, and transitivity combines the two covering refinements.

= iv
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

First prove the hinted statement. Let $U\subseteq y_n$ be the domain of a partial presheaf map $\phi:U\to y_{n+1}$. If $U$ is nonempty, take a section $f:m\to n$ in $U(m)$ and write $g=\phi_m(f):m\to n+1$. Hypothesis (c) supplies $h,k:m+1\to m$ with $fh=fk$ but $gh\ne gk$. Because $U$ is a subpresheaf, both restrictions belong to $U(m+1)$; naturality gives
$$
gh=\phi_{m+1}(fh)=\phi_{m+1}(fk)=gk,
$$
a contradiction. Therefore **every such partial map has empty domain**.

Now suppose a presheaf map $y_n\to A_{n+1}$ existed. Pull back the <monomorphism> $y_{n+1}\hookrightarrow A_{n+1}$ along it. Its unit image is dense by the preceding plus-construction argument, and density is stable under pullback, so the resulting domain $U\subseteq y_n$ is dense. It gives exactly a partial map $U\to y_{n+1}$ of the prohibited kind. Such a domain cannot be empty: at the section $1_n$ of $y_n(n)$, density would otherwise require the empty <sieve on a category> to cover. Thus there is no map $y_n\to A_{n+1}$.

Any sheaf map $A_n\to A_{n+1}$, composed with $y_n\hookrightarrow A_n$, would be such a presheaf map. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{Sh}(C,J)}(A_n,A_{n+1})=\varnothing.}
$$
The stronger intermediate statement also says $A_{n+1}(n)=\varnothing$, by the Yoneda correspondence. It will be useful for the last clause.

= v
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The sheaf $A_n$ is noninitial: it contains $y_n$, whose value at $n$ includes $1_n$. In a <two-valued topos> its support, the image of $A_n\to1$, can therefore only be $1$. An image equal to $0$ would force $A_n=0$, since the <initial object> of a topos is strict. Consequently **every $A_n\to1$ is an <epimorphism>**.

Products of sheaves on a small site exist and are computed as products of the underlying presheaves, since a product of unique matching-family amalgamations again uniquely amalgamates. For the countable product $P=\prod_{n\in\mathbb N}A_n$, its value at stage $m$ is $\prod_nA_n(m)$. The factor with $n=m+1$ is empty by the stronger statement proved in the preceding part. Thus $P(m)=\varnothing$ at every stage $m$, with the unique empty restriction maps. It is the empty presheaf, already an initial sheaf because no empty <sieve on a category> covers. Hence
$$
\boxed{A_n\twoheadrightarrow1\text{ for every }n,\qquad\prod_{n\in\mathbb N}A_n\cong0.}
$$
This proves the final PDF-only request and exhibits the <separating tower site with vanishing product>. It is a genuine failure of countable choice in this Boolean two-valued <sheaf topos>; it does not contradict the classical external construction of the site.
